<H3><CENTER><FONT COLOR="#ffff00"><A HREF="mailto:roy.tomlinson@virginnet.co.uk">Roy Tomlinson</A></FONT></CENTER></H3>

        Roy has a 1992 (J registered) 16v 5dr Tipo with 63,000 miles on the clock which
        he has had for the last 3 years. He loves Tipos so much he recently
        bought a cheap 1987 1.4 Tipo for travelling to work in as he
        couldn't bear to be sensible and sell the 16v for a newer 'normal'
        car. The car is standard apart from a performance air filter.
        He is however thinking about a Supersprint exhaust.<P>

        Roy recently had to buy a new ECU after a recurrent damp starting
        problem was tracked down to water entering the unit at some point.
        Beware! Fiat wanted over &pound;900 for a new one. He eventually
        tracked a Weber agent down near to him and got an exchange unit
        for &pound;336 - bit of a difference.<P>

        According to the Fiat technician who services Roy's car the best way
        to make the car really perform and blow everything else of the road
        is to go turbo. You can now pick up Lancia Thema 16v Turbo's for
        less than &pound;2500 which are the same engine but with a lower
        compression. The engine management system etc can all be swapped
        over. 210 BHP sounds tempting. Roy may consider this if his
        engine fails. After hearing that 16v twin cams can shed belts before
        the recommended change time Roy had his replaced at 46,000.
        <P>
        Roy's not had a lot of luck recently. After replacing the ECU the
        steering rack broke.
	<P>
	In June 2000 Roy purchased a second hand (well two week old) set of
	Punto HGT wheels for &pound;230 including new nuts, locking nuts,
	carriage, balancing and fitting. The owner has brought a set of Fiat
	aftermarket wheels and didn't want the orginals any more.
	<P>
	At 75,000 miles the clutch slave cylinder failed.
	<P>
	In August 2000 Roy had a new radiator fitted, belts, water pump etc
	etc etc. Ouch! The front plastic mesh on the grill was looking
	worse for wear especially after finding a chestnut lodged in it
	one day. Roy managed to cut the old plastic mesh off still leaving
	the mounting clips and pressed the new stainless steel mesh into
	place. This he picked up for &pound;2 from a hardware store.
	He has secured it with some black silicone sealant glue costing
	&pound;8.

<P>
